WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Work in the domain A generated
over the integers by i,

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.420
the chosen roots for the distinct primes
dividing u, and the reciprocal of u.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:14.020
Here u is odd and greater than one,
and A sits inside the complex numbers.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.520
The exact divided differences
x sub S already belong to A by

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.740
Node seven point two: divide
coefficients exactly, evaluate,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.340
then multiply by the
unit inverse of c sub S.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.160
For either family, the product
of one plus t x sub S over even

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.540
subsets equals the product over odd
subsets. Expand both sides exactly.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.660
Each has a constant one, a linear
term t times its own sum of x values,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.760
and a remainder divisible by t squared.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.320
The two remainders are kept, not
discarded as an approximation.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.740
Subtract the odd-subset expansion
from the even-subset expansion.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.520
The two constant terms cancel.
Gather the linear terms into t L,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.520
and the difference of the
remainders into t squared Q.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.670
We obtain t L plus t
squared Q equals zero.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.920
L is the alternating sum of the x values,

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.640
with plus signs for even subsets
and minus signs for odd subsets.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.940
Both L and Q belong to A. Each
family has its own L and Q.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.830
For the r family, t equals two.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.500
For the w family, t equals one plus i.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.340
Both are nonzero in the original domain.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.050
Factor the equality as t times the
quantity L plus t Q equals zero.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.760
Cancel the nonzero scalar there, obtaining
the exact equality L equals minus t Q.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:51.180
This uses the domain property; it
does not require t to be a unit.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.740
No residue has been divided yet.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.460
Only now reduce modulo the ideal
generated by one plus i in A.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.740
Since two equals the product
of one plus i and one minus i,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.070
both choices of t have residue zero.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:11.770
The right-hand side minus t Q
vanishes, so L is congruent to zero.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.000
Both equations reduce in
the same quotient ring.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.180
That quotient need not
be a field or a domain.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:25.860
Write c sub S for c evaluated at zeta
sub S, and similarly for d and g.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.460
For the first family, the alternating
sum of d sub S minus c sub S,

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.980
divided by two c sub S,
is congruent to zero.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.200
For the second, replace that
numerator by g sub S minus c sub S,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.660
and the divisor by the product
of one plus i with c sub S.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.520
This sum is also congruent to zero.
These fractions denote existing elements

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.900
of A formed before reduction,
not division by zero residues.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.300
If we reduced the original
product equality first,

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.480
every factor one plus t
x sub S would become one.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.820
We would see only one equals one
and lose the linear information.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.960
The useful order is exact expansion,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.240
subtraction, cancellation
in A, and then reduction.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.880
For u equal to three, the two subsets
give just one factor on each side.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:24.080
Both remainders vanish, so Q equals zero
and the alternating sums are zero exactly.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:29.280
The general argument keeps the remainders
and obtains the two congruences we need.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.620
The next step adds them term by
term in the same quotient ring.
